**Theorem 6.3 (full expansion and coverage of all near-endpoint parameters).**
The exact original value satisfies the first line of EP.9 for EVERY
sufficiently small positive $\varepsilon$. The contacts and masses have
expansions

$$
\begin{aligned}
d_j&=-\frac{b_j}{\Gamma}\sqrt\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon),\\
\pi_j&=\frac14+\frac{2w_j}{\Gamma}\sqrt\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon).
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.42}
$$

Proof. All expectations in this proof use the four exact coefficient
masses $\pi_j(p)$. From EP.17, with $d=|u|-1$,
$\mathbb Ed=-\mathbb Ed^2/2$ and
$\tau=1-\mathbb Ed^2/2$. Also $\mathbb Ek=-B/4-\mathbb E\mathfrak r$.
Consequently the EXACT identity for EP.24 is

$$
W(p)=1-\frac B4p-\frac12\mathbb Ed^2-p\mathbb E\mathfrak r.
\tag{EP.43}
$$

Write $d_j=-\ell_jp+t_jp^2+O(p^3)$. Use EP.36 and EP.40 to expand
EP.43. At cubic order the unknown $t_j$ terms are
$\sum_j\ell_jt_j/4$ from $-\mathbb Ed^2/2$ and its negative from
$-p\mathbb E\mathfrak r$. They cancel. The weight terms combine to
$\sum_jw_j\ell_j^2/2$, and the quadratic residual contributes
$-\sum_jD_j\ell_j^2/8$. Thus

$$
W(p)=1-\frac B4p+v_2p^2+v_3p^3+O(p^4),
\tag{EP.44}
$$

with the fully evaluated $v_2,v_3$ in EP.34. No unknown second-order
contact solution remains in this coefficient.

There is also the exact envelope identity $W'(p)=K(p)$. To verify it,
differentiate $W=\sum_j\pi_j(1+d_j+p k_j(d_j))$. Replace contact
values and tangents by $a\cdot m_j$ and $a\cdot m_j'$ using EP.20.
The terms from differentiating weights and contacts sum to
$a\cdot(d/dp)\sum_j\pi_jm_j(d_j)=0$. The remaining term is $K$.
Analyticity therefore gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
K(p)&=-B/4+2v_2p+3v_3p^2+O(p^3),\\
\varepsilon(p)=1-\tau(p)&=1-W+pW'
                     =v_2p^2+2v_3p^3+O(p^4).
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.45}
$$

Because $v_2>0$, $\varepsilon'(p)>0$ for small positive $p$.
The positive branch
$\sqrt\varepsilon=p\sqrt{v_2+2v_3p+O(p^2)}$ is analytic in $p$
with derivative $\sqrt{v_2}\ne0$ at zero, and has a unique analytic
inverse in $\sqrt\varepsilon$. It covers an entire positive endpoint
neighborhood, rather than just a sequence or a formal trajectory.
Its inverse expansion is

$$
p=\frac{\sqrt\varepsilon}{\sqrt{v_2}}
       -\frac{v_3}{v_2^2}\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^{3/2}).
\tag{EP.46}
$$

Substituting into $T_{\rm flat}+K(p)$ gives
$T_0+2\sqrt{v_2}\sqrt\varepsilon+(v_3/v_2)\varepsilon
+O(\varepsilon^{3/2})$, which is EP.9 and EP.35. Substitution in
EP.36 gives EP.42. All remainders follow from analytic functions on
a sufficiently small interval; no quantified radius or error constant
is inferred. Shrink that interval so its contrasts lie in the original
residual interval. This completes the parameter-coverage part of
Theorem 1.4. $\square$

